[Annotator "Opera development audit, 2026-09-21"]

1. e4 { Occupies the centre and frees both the queen and the f1 bishop. This is preparation for useful piece activity, not pawn motion for its own sake. Audit at 3 seconds: Opera (Morphy) d4; standard evaluator e4. } 1... e5 2. Nf3 { Develops a new piece with a threat against e5. Development and a concrete demand on the opponent occur together. Audit at 3 seconds: Opera (Morphy) Nf3; standard evaluator Nc3. } 2... d6 3. d4 { Challenges the centre while opening lines for the remaining army. Nc3 is also a sound developing choice here. Audit at 3 seconds: Opera (Morphy) Nc3; standard evaluator Nc3. } 3... Bg4 4. dxe5 { Uses the central tension concretely. The pin of the f3 knight to the queen does not prevent this pawn capture; tactics decide whether the pin matters. Audit at 3 seconds: Opera (Morphy) Nc3; standard evaluator dxe5. } 4... Bxf3 5. Qxf3 { Recaptures without breaking the kingside pawns and puts the queen where it can cooperate with a bishop on c4. The early queen move has a specific purpose. Audit at 3 seconds: Opera (Morphy) Qxf3; standard evaluator Qxf3. } 5... dxe5 6. Bc4 { Adds a second attacker toward f7. The useful feature is the shared target and access to the king, rather than merely moving another minor piece. Audit at 3 seconds: Opera (Morphy) Bc4; standard evaluator Qb3. } 6... Nf6 7. Qb3 { Creates threats against f7 and b7. After Qe7, the black queen has a defensive job and obstructs the f8 bishop. Audit at 3 seconds: Opera (Morphy) Qb3; standard evaluator Qb3. } 7... Qe7 8. Nc3 { Brings another piece into play while keeping the initiative. Qxb7 is also objectively strong; this choice is a style preference, not a universal prohibition on taking pawns. Audit at 3 seconds: Opera (Morphy) Nc3; standard evaluator Bxf7+. } 8... c6 9. Bg5 { Completes deployment of the surviving minor pieces and pins the f6 knight to the queen on e7. The rooks still need activation. Audit at 3 seconds: Opera (Morphy) Bg5; standard evaluator a4. } 9... b5 10. Nxb5 { Offers the c3 knight to remove b5 and draw the c6 pawn away from the diagonal toward e8. The move remains strong against defenses that decline the sacrifice. Audit at 3 seconds: Opera (Morphy) Nxb5; standard evaluator Bxf6. } 10... cxb5 11. Bxb5+ { Uses the opened diagonal with check. Black has several legal replies; Nbd7 is the historical reply, not a forced move. Audit at 3 seconds: Opera (Morphy) Bxb5+; standard evaluator Bxb5+. } 11... Nbd7 12. O-O-O { Places the king safely on c1 and immediately activates a rook on the open d-file. The wing is selected for the needs of this position. Audit at 3 seconds: Opera (Morphy) O-O-O; standard evaluator O-O-O. } 12... Rd8 13. Rxd7 { Removes the d7 blocker with an exchange sacrifice. If the f6 knight recaptures, the bishop on g5 can take the queen on e7. Black has alternatives to the historical Rxd7. Audit at 3 seconds: Opera (Morphy) Rxd7; standard evaluator Rxd7. } 13... Rxd7 14. Rd1 { Brings the last rook into the operation. The quiet move renews pressure against a pinned defender and makes every remaining attacking piece useful. Audit at 3 seconds: Opera (Morphy) Rd1; standard evaluator Rd1. } 14... Qe6 15. Bxd7+ { Trades the b5 bishop to clear the d-file while retaining the g5 bishop that will cover the mating squares. Qxd7 is a stronger defense than the historical Nxd7, although White still wins. Audit at 3 seconds: Opera (Morphy) Bxd7+; standard evaluator Bxd7+. } 15... Nxd7 16. Qb8+ { Deflects the knight away from d8. Nxb8 is the only legal reply and Rd8 is then checkmate. Audit at 3 seconds: Opera (Morphy) Qb8+; standard evaluator Qb8+. } 16... Nxb8 17. Rd8# { The remaining rook gives mate. The bishop on g5 protects d8 and denies the e7 flight square; Black’s own pieces close other routes. Audit at 3 seconds: Opera (Morphy) Rd8#; standard evaluator Rd8#. } 1-0
